her in the low ranks where nobody''s really going to punish the fact that she doesn''t really have a play\r\n40:50\r\nstyle. And then up top where free damage isn''t enough and you actually have to be creative to land the alt and people will\r\n40:58\r\npunish that alt cuz they can see it coming from a mile away. We drop the 37% win rate. It''s sad she has nothing left\r\n41:05\r\nwhen she goes up to GM and celestial and I don''t know if people even pick her beyond that. Next up, we have DPS pool.\r\nDPSpool\r\n41:12\r\nAnd again, flat to mid-40s. You''re noticing a pattern here that the DPS players for the most part don''t have\r\n41:18\r\namazing win rates. With DPS pool, we mostly have 43 to 46% through the low\r\n41:24\r\nranks and then 47% in GM and 45% in celestial. Yawn. Again, same issue as\r\n41:30\r\nthe other dead pools. It''s funny that regardless of whether it''s a tank pool, a DPS pool, or a heal pool, at the end\r\n41:37\r\nof the day, you got to stay up. You got to be purposeful with that alt. You got to know your upgrades, and people seemingly don''t. Next up, we have Black\r\nBlack Widow\r\n41:44\r\nWidow. Now, after the rework, the data that we''re seeing here is not really\r\n41:50\r\nhelpful, but probably better than what she''s had before. She''s at 43 to 45% win\r\n41:55\r\nrate through the low ranks and then 45% in GM and 48% in celestial. It will be\r\n42:01\r\nreally interesting to see how that changes or doesn''t in the coming weeks. She is basically yet another poke DPS\r\n42:08\r\nand I see people doing some pretty cool stuff with her. I see her in game now and my mindset''s changing. She''s no\r\n42:14\r\nlonger just a throw pick. She can present some danger. I am really crossing my fingers for her. I want this\r\n42:20\r\ncharacter to get some respect. I want her to get her flowers. I hate the fact that she''s always been the throw pick\r\n42:27\r\nsignal and I don''t like that. I thought she was a really cool character before. So, I''m hoping that this climbs up more\r\n42:33\r\nand more and we get to see her outperform the Helas and the Phoenix''s of the world and, you know, get the\r\n42:38\r\nspotlight for once. Next up, we have Squirrel Girl. Yeah, a catastrophic\r\nSquirrel Girl\r\n42:44\r\ntopend collapse. Mid-40s through the low ranks, cratering to 38% in GM, and\r\n42:52\r\n[clears throat] 31% in Celestial. My god, this is the\r\n42:57\r\nsingle worst number in the whole game. She can be a zoning menace when she''s\r\n43:03\r\npiloted well. She can bounce chaos off the walls. And they do this thing where they''ll bounce a nut off of the ceiling\r\n43:10\r\nand by the time it falls back down, they''ll throw another one where it''s going to fall. And there''s some squirrel\r\n43:15\r\ngirls out there that are just demons, but not the ones in Celestial. The problem is that again, like Hawkeye,\r\n43:21\r\nlike Bucky, she is very divable. She is very punishable. Her escapes are many\r\n43:27\r\nbut slow. And you know, you can shut her down if she''s really causing a lot of trouble. In the low ranks, people are\r\n43:33\r\nnot going to, you know, switch or know how to counter her, so naturally the win rate is acceptable. But then in the\r\n43:38\r\nhigher ranks, they just slap her down. Next up, we have Cyclops. And well, he has a bottom tier at almost every rank.\r\nCyclops\r\n43:45\r\n43 to 45% win rate through the low ranks and then 42% in GM and then 43% in\r\n43:50\r\ncelestial. I don''t know what''s going on, you know. Now, don''t let that deter you. The character is extremely strong. These\r\n43:57\r\nnumbers are not great. They''re also not the worst in the game. But I guess it might be a case of people wanting to\r\n44:03\r\nplay the fairly new and strong character and then not knowing how to pilot him. Chances are we''re waiting for some kind\r\n44:10\r\nof a another buff or some kind of a meta shift to really enable him, but I think he''s strong enough as it is. And you\r\n44:16\r\nknow, play him, I guess. Next up, we have Phoenix. And similar story to\r\nPhoenix\r\n44:21\r\nCyclops, which is applicable given that they''re boyfriend and girlfriend. We got her in the low 40s all throughout from\r\n44:29\r\nbronze up until celestial. On paper, she should be dominating. And all I keep hearing is how good she is and how\r\n44:36\r\ndominant she is. But apparently in those ranks, she''s not. Could it be that the\r\n44:41\r\nslopfest out there of head scans is actually just oppressive and annoying, but doesn''t really dominate games? I\r\n44:48\r\ndon''t know. It seems like that might be the case. And finally, we have Moonnight, the other carrier of the\r\nMoon Knight\r\n44:55\r\ngraveyard number. Mid40s through the low ranks and then collapses to 38% in GM\r\n45:01\r\nand 33% in celestial. Yeah, he''s kind of losing his identity. He does pose\r\n45:08\r\nproblems at low elo for sure, especially if you flank and have good timing with\r\n45:13\r\nyour attacks as well as the alt. But then just like the other quote unquote beginner character, he gets wrecked in\r\n45:20\r\nthe higher ranks. I know there are people that are very cracked in Moonight, but honestly, I haven''t seen\r\n45:25\r\none for quite some time that''s that good. I usually see one on a team, they kind of underperform, they do a bunch of\r\n45:31\r\ndamage, don''t get a lot of final hits, and then die a lot. Now, here''s the thing about win rate. On\r\nTHE META MAP - Busted, Sleepers, Traps & Dead\r\n45:38\r\ntheir own, they can kind of lie to you, and so can pick rates. A hero can post a\r\n45:44\r\ngorgeous win rate, but their player base is quite niche. A hero can be picked in\r\n45:49\r\nevery single game and quietly lose you all of them. Cloak and dagger. Ultimately, neither number means much\r\n45:57\r\njust by itself. But put them together, how often a hero gets picked against how\r\n46:03\r\noften it actually wins, and now you can see what a hero really is. So that''s\r\n46:09\r\nwhat this is on the screen. a few lines on a map. Pick rate one way, win rate\r\n46:14\r\nthe other way, and it carves the whole roster into four boxes. Top right, we\r\n46:19\r\nhave the oppressive characters, the people with high pick rates and high win rates, and we have 11 bad boys and girls\r\n46:26\r\nthere. These are, as of this week, the meta''s problem children. Everyone plays\r\n46:31\r\nthem and they still win, which is the exact combo that gets a hero nerfed. Rocket Raccoon, for example, is picked\r\n46:38\r\nin nearly one in five games and wins 56% of them. Magneto is picked in a quarter\r\n46:44\r\nof all games and still clears 50% win rate. Mantis and Penny live up there too\r\n46:50\r\non raw win rate alone with The Thing, Thor, Hulk, Hela, Black Panther, Magic, and Venom filling it all out. So, if you\r\n46:57\r\nwant to know what''s genuinely unarguably strong right now this week, this is the\r\n47:03\r\nbox. No debate. Top left is the sleeper box. Those are the characters with low\r\n47:08\r\npick rate and high win rate. Here we have 15 heroes and this is the one you\r\n47:14\r\nshould really be staring at because this is free elo. Ultron for example is winning 59% while barely anyone touches\r\n47:22\r\nhim. Storm is at 56% plus Iron Man, Psylock, Groot, Black Cat, and\r\n47:29\r\nDaredevil. I guess being unfashionable and undesirable is a real competitive advantage, huh, Storm? Bottom right is\r\n47:36\r\nthe trap box. We have high pick rates and low win rates here and we have 17\r\n47:43\r\nheroes, the biggest box in the game. Cloak and Dagger is picked in a full quarter of those games and winning 48%.\r\n47:50\r\nWhite Fox is at 18% pick rate and 47% win rate. and then Invisible Woman,\r\n47:57\r\nDoctor Strange, Amrost, Luna Snow, all very heavily played. All under 50% win\r\n48:03\r\nrate. Bottom left is the dead box. Here we''re talking low pick and low win, and\r\n48:09\r\nwe have 11 heroes in it. This is the graveyard. Moonight at 41% win rate,\r\n48:17\r\nSquirrel Girl at 43% win rate, Hold Your Horses, Phoenix at 42% win rate, all\r\n48:25\r\nthree Deadpool variants, Scarlet Witch, and unfortunately Wolverine. Notice the\r\n48:30\r\npattern here before we move on to the next segment. The people screaming, \"Nerf this one and Nerf that one,\" are\r\n48:37\r\nalways pointing to the top right, while the people quietly climbing are living there in the top left playing the stuff\r\n48:43\r\nnobody''s complaining about yet. Now, let''s talk about the roll war. I\r\nTHE ROLE WAR - Tank vs DPS vs Support\r\n48:50\r\nwas wondering now that I have all these delicious statistics at my fingertips, which role wins the most. Well, let me\r\n48:57\r\ngive you the answer before I explain it. Tanks win the most across every rank\r\n49:03\r\nthat matters. The tank roll has the highest average win rate in the game this week. Supports come in second,\r\n49:10\r\nsteady down the middle, and then DPS, the role that everyone fights to lock,\r\n49:15\r\nis on average the flakiest and lowest winning of the three. What''s that? Knock\r\n49:21\r\nknock. It''s a sink at the door. Let it in. Now, let''s get a little bit more granular. Here''s the shape across the\r\n49:28\r\nentire ladder. At bronze, it''s basically a coin flip. Tanks win about 50% of the\r\n49:33\r\ntime, supports win about 50% of the time, and DPS just under at 48%. Silver\r\n49:39\r\nis the one weird rank where everything bunches up and support briefly noses in\r\n49:44\r\nfront. But from gold onward, the tank line just pulls away. 51% at gold, 51%\r\n49:52\r\nalmost 52% at diamond, 52% at celestial, climbing the whole way. DPS goes the\r\n50:00\r\nother direction, 49.9% basically 50% at gold and sliding down to 48% at\r\n50:06\r\ncelestial. The gap between the best roll and the worst starts at a point and a half in bronze and then widens to over\r\n50:14\r\nfour points at the top. So, uh, let''s ask the question that everybody''s wondering right now. Why does the role\r\n50:20\r\neveryone dreads keep winning? Well, there''s a few theories. A tank''s job\r\n50:25\r\nscales with the lobby, holding space, enabling your team. These are nearly\r\n50:31\r\nworthless in bronze where nobody''s going to take that space. [laughter] There''s no team to enable. However, as your\r\n50:37\r\nteammates get better and smarter, that space you create finally gets used.\r\n50:44\r\nMeanwhile, DPS gets harder as you climb and not easier. Down low, you can farm\r\n50:50\r\nplayers who don''t respect angles, don''t know how to position, but up top, the enemy tank is holding that space against\r\n50:56\r\nyou, buddy. And let''s not forget, DPS is the most crowded role in the game.\r\n51:01\r\nEveryone wants it. So, the average DPS player is more likely to be kind of\r\n51:06\r\n[ __ ] And supports have just a safety net, right? Even a mediocre healer adds\r\n51:12\r\nvalue by just freaking existing, which is why it never really bottoms out. So, with these discoveries, here''s a message\r\n51:19\r\nto my fellow tanks. I know the role is difficult. It feels like a grind and it\r\n51:25\r\nfeels thankless. You''re eating pressure and you still get blamed. I know it sucks. But at the end of the day, the\r\n51:33\r\nsame thing that doesn''t reflect your value, the scoreboard, is the thing that shows that you do have value and you do\r\n51:41\r\nwin more than anyone else. So, if you''re stuck maybe as a DPS and want to climb,\r\n51:46\r\nI don''t know, man, maybe consider learning how to tank. Spoiler alert, it''s not just soaking in damage and\r\n51:53\r\npressing W. It''s not that simple. Now,\r\nBEST ONE-TRICKS\r\n51:58\r\nwho are the one tricks? Huh? Penny Parker for tank, magic for DPS, and\r\n52:04\r\nRocket for support. Those are the three best one tricks in the game right now based on data. And it really genuinely\r\n52:11\r\nhelps that all three are stupidly fun to play. Here''s the thing that people get wrong, though. A one-trick pick is a\r\n52:18\r\ncompletely different question than a tier list. A tier list asks, \"What''s strongest if you can pick anything?\" One\r\n52:26\r\ntricking asks, \"What survives when you cannot adapt?\" And that''s the only condition under which one tricking is\r\n52:32\r\nacceptable. Consistency. When you play one hero all the time, every game, you\r\n52:38\r\nstop losing to unfamiliarity. You know the cooldowns. You know the angles. You know the exact frame when\r\n52:45\r\nyou need to be committing because you''ve been in that spot 500 [ __ ] times. Over time, you can learn where penny\r\n52:52\r\nnests work and where they don''t. You can''t learn that from just a couple of games. Over time, your game sense with\r\n52:58\r\nmagic will improve, and you''ll be able to switch between frontlinining and diving like a pro. And over time, you\r\n53:04\r\nwill discover that Rocket Raccoon is a complicated character, more complicated\r\n53:09\r\nthan people think. Oh, and a quick one for you guys, because this statistic has not moved in\r\n53:16\r\na year, and it won''t move next week either. The best team comp in Marvel Rivals, according to the data, is 222.\r\nBEST TEAM COMP\r\n53:24\r\ntwo tanks, two DPS, two supports, and it''s not even close. It''s the highest\r\n53:29\r\nwin rate and the most picked comp at every single rank. At Diamond, it''s 67%\r\n53:36\r\nof all games. At Grandmaster, it''s 71. At Celestial, 74. And this ties directly\r\n53:43\r\nback to the value of tanks. Almost every tank in this game dies the second they''re left alone. Only a few can\r\n53:51\r\nsurvive solo. So, the game is not magically punishing you for picking the wrong composition out of nowhere. It''s\r\n53:58\r\npunishing you because one tank physically cannot hold a line the way two can. It''s pretty simple. 222 wins\r\n54:06\r\nbecause the roster was built for two tanks. Everything else is you fighting the game''s own math.\r\nTLDR\r\n54:14\r\nNow, if you''re actually trying to climb this week and the week after and the week after, here''s what the numbers are\r\n54:21\r\ntelling you to do, and it''s three things to be specific. The first one is if you want free ELO, live in the sleeper box.\r\n54:28\r\nUltron, Storm, a dozen others. Nobody''s really playing them and they''re quietly\r\n54:35\r\nwinning. Being the unfashionable one is a competitive advantage. Let everybody\r\n54:40\r\nelse fight over Cloak and Dagger. The second one is if you''re going to one trick, marry consistency, not hype.\r\n54:47\r\nPenny, Magic, and Rocket are the safe commitments. They have a win rate you can rely on game after game, map after\r\n54:55\r\nmap, not a hero that spikes this week and then vanishes next. Pick one, learn\r\n55:00\r\nit cold, and then let the reps do the work. And number three, for the love of freaking god, run two tanks and kill the\r\n55:08\r\ndeployables. That''s it. That''s the whole game below diamond. And also, please, for the love of God, stay alive. Now,\r\n55:15\r\nbear in mind that these numbers are not gospel. I''ll be doing these videos every single week. So, if anything, they''re a\r\n55:22\r\nbit of a foggy mirror. That is the Nerf dict for this week. Congratulations. You\r\n55:27\r\ngot Nerf [ __ ] for the first time. The next one''s going to be next Friday. I\r\n55:32\r\nwonder if anything''s going to change. Who knows? I''ll see you in the next one. And hey, until then, if you need some\r\n55:38\r\nmore Nerf Bull in your life, more Nerf Bull is coming in three, two, [ __ ] you.\r\n55:45\r\n[music]","gameplayArchetypes":{"peni-parker":[{"role":"Vanguard","label":"Zone Tank","description":"Controls space through nests and deployables; her value drops quickly when opponents clear that setup.","sourceTitle":"Nerfpool: Every Hero''s Win Rate (Season 9, Week 1)","sourceUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VB6OIA-ChmA&t=31s","timestamp":"0:31"}],"the-thing":[{"role":"Vanguard","label":"Brawl Tank","description":"A durable f